      "id": 1,
      "text": "Thomas Pell bought the land from the Siwanoy Indians in 1654, founding the Manor of Pelham that still shapes the town’s layout.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pelham%2C_New_York",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1975, North Pelham and Pelham villages merged, creating the current Village of Pelham that governs the area today.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pelham%2C_New_York",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Pelham Picture House, one of America’s oldest single‑screen theaters, earned a spot on the National Register of Historic Places in 2010.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8,
        13,
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pelham%2C_New_York",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Pelham’s downtown is a pedestrian‑friendly hub where shops, restaurants, town hall, and the Metro‑North station sit within a short stroll.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5,
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.visitwestchesterny.com/discover/communities/southern-westchester/pelham/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "After 1916, the Sanborn Map Company headquartered in Pelham produced detailed fire‑insurance maps that mapped the nation’s cities.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pelham%2C_New_York",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Established by New York State in 1788, Pelham became part of Westchester County, placing it just 10 miles northeast of Midtown Manhattan.",
